From 3f0efe209aa7fc3bc2e6e767cc262943328da950 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 06:55:40 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] ActiveAE: answer a lost display in every state

CActiveAE::OnLostDisplay() waits up to five seconds for the engine to
acknowledge, on the thread that changes the display mode. Three states
answer (waiting for the preconditions, the error state, configured).
While the engine drains for a reconfiguration that a change of an audio
setting asked for, the message fell through to the top state, was
logged as not handled and never answered: the GUI stood still for those
five seconds.

The top state now acknowledges it, for that state and for any other
that does not act on it. The sink was not suspended there before
either; what changes is that the change of mode no longer waits out the
reconfiguration but overlaps it.

The Intel Dolby Vision LibreELEC fork (CroqueMr/libreelec-x86-DV,
d9ad989ee928, published as R1.0.0 Beta3) made the same change. No
sequence that leads there in ordinary use is known: a hardening.
---
 xbmc/cores/AudioEngine/Engines/ActiveAE/ActiveAE.cpp | 5 +++++
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+)

diff --git a/xbmc/cores/AudioEngine/Engines/ActiveAE/ActiveAE.cpp b/xbmc/cores/AudioEngine/Engines/ActiveAE/ActiveAE.cpp
index 893956c..37a79d5 100644
--- a/xbmc/cores/AudioEngine/Engines/ActiveAE/ActiveAE.cpp
+++ b/xbmc/cores/AudioEngine/Engines/ActiveAE/ActiveAE.cpp
@@ -405,6 +405,11 @@ void CActiveAE::StateMachine(int signal, Protocol *port, Message *msg)
           return;
         case CActiveAEControlProtocol::DISPLAYRESET:
           return;
+        case CActiveAEControlProtocol::DISPLAYLOST:
+          // a state that does not act on it (unconfigured, draining to reconfigure) still
+          // answers: OnLostDisplay() waits for this on the thread that changes the mode
+          msg->Reply(CActiveAEControlProtocol::ACC);
+          return;
         case CActiveAEControlProtocol::APPFOCUSED:
           m_sink.m_controlPort.SendOutMessage(CSinkControlProtocol::APPFOCUSED, msg->data, sizeof(bool));
           return;
